<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#haveli">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haveli</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A haveli is a traditional townhouse or mansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, built around one or more inner courtyards. Wealthy merchant families, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, decorated their havelis with carved stone or wooden facades, balconies and wall paintings. Famous examples survive in Jaisalmer, the Shekhawati region and the old cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haveli.jpg" alt="Haveli" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Taveneaux · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Patwon_ki_haveli_18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word haveli comes from the Arabic hawali, meaning partition or private space, and was popularised under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> without any architectural meaning. Early havelis served the Muslim rulers of the subcontinent and became an important part of Mughal cities, blending local and Islamic traditions. Houses built round courtyards are far older in the region, with early examples from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> some Vaishnava sects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a> kingdoms also called their temples havelis, before the word came to mean the townhouses of the merchant class.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The heart of a haveli is the chowk or courtyard, around which family life revolved. It worked as a lightwell and ventilated the house in a hot, dry climate, while arcades or high walls kept the rooms cool. The courtyard could separate the quarters of men and women: in Shekhawati a typical haveli had an outer court for men and an inner one for women, and the largest had three or four courts on two or three storeys. Common materials were brick, sandstone, marble, wood, plaster and granite. The sacred tulsi plant was often kept in the courtyard, which also served for weddings and festivals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>For the Marwaris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> havelis were status symbols and homes for extended families, closed on all sides with one large gate. Between 1830 and 1930 Marwari merchants built havelis in Shekhawati and Marwar and had them painted by artists working under Mughal influence. In Jaisalmer the merchants' houses were carved in yellow sandstone with jharokhas, or balconies. The first there, Patwon Ji ki Haveli, is a cluster of five houses begun in 1805 by Guman Chand Patwa, a trader in jewellery and brocades, one for each of his five sons. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> the Haveli of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nau-nihal-singh">Nau Nihal Singh</a>, of the mid-19th century, is the only Sikh-era haveli that keeps its original ornament.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haveli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
